A||N||10||shawnster84||Control Arm bolts||||5||
Z||000000||shawnster84||10-03-2001||07:20 PM||||I'm trying to remove the upper control arm and I cannot get the long bolt out!!! I do have a sawzall but I wanted to use that as a last resort..any suggestions??||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||10-03-2001||11:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by shawnster84:<BR><B>I'm trying to remove the upper control arm and I cannot get the long bolt out!!! I do have a sawzall but I wanted to use that as a last resort..any suggestions??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Shawn!<BR>I use a penatrant called pb blaster...<BR>Can you rotate the bolt?<BR>Normally, they get quite stuck from the rust, but keep working it and spraying it till it comes loose...<BR>If not, you will need a replacement front axle...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroHarry||10-03-2001||11:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>If not, you will need a replacement front axle...<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Okay you lost me, axles came in where...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||10-03-2001||11:32 PM||||That bolt is about 6-7" long and pretty unique to the Fiero.  I don't know if you can get a replacement from a dealer (they'll probably want an arm and a leg for one anyway).  If you have to cut it off you'll probably have to find one off another Fiero in a boneyard.<P>Spray it with penetrating oils and keep turning it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||shawnster84||10-04-2001||06:23 AM||||Oh yeah..the bolt turns good, its just i can't slide it out of the control arm..I'll try spraying it with pb blaster and see if that helps..Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Leadfoot||10-04-2001||09:56 AM||||I used a torch and cold chisel on all my bolts...front and on the cradle.  The bushing change has got to be one of the most PIA repairs I have ever done.  Good luck! ||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-04-2001||11:04 AM||||I replaced mine recently, It costs about $18. Canadian a bolt ( about 12 us?) <P><BR>they usually are very stuck in there.. put oil on it and keep prying back and forth..<P>Dont forget to torque the bolts back to the right pressure...  When I had mine replaced a few years back, they never torqed them..the bolts wore out in about 6 months of daily driving..<P><BR>If you dont know the torque settings, PM me to remind me and I will post it hen I get home.<P>good luck!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||10-04-2001||06:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroHarry:<BR><B> Okay you lost me, axles came in where...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, Ya got me...<BR>In the old days, the assembly that crosses from side to side was called an axle...<BR>It is not a part of the frame, but bolts to the sub-frame...<BR>It is called the front crossmember assembly in the service manual...<BR>Just about everything bolts to it...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fierowrecker||10-04-2001||06:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by shawnster84:<BR><B>Oh yeah..the bolt turns good, its just i can't slide it out of the control arm..I'll try spraying it with pb blaster and see if that helps..Thanks!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Shawn!<BR>Look very carefully at the bushings when you turn the bolt...<BR>If the sleaves turn with the bolt, then it is time to torch out the rubber, and open the sleaves with chissels (or screw drivers!)...<BR>Keep a spray bottle of water handy to douse the flames, I add a squirt of soap to make it "wet" better...<BR>I just use a cheap propane torch to burn out the bushings...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000009||shawnster84||10-04-2001||07:23 PM||||one of the bushings turn but the other one is so tore up, its just hanging there..I dont understand why you have to burn the bushings away? is it because they are "fused" to the bolt?..I wish they would have put some kind of shield or something on the control bolts when they built the car..what a PITA!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||10-04-2001||08:44 PM||||no no, see there is a metal  sleeve down the center of the bushing, it allows the bushing to pivot on the bolt. Over time it has a tendancy to rust to the bolt and it is too large to fit thru the bolt hole. There is a seperation in the sleeve, and if you can burn out the bushing, then place a flat screwdrive into the seperation and pound with a BFH and try to seperate the sleeve. I had this same problem...||||reg||1
